", "closed_by": null, "comments": [{"author": "person-38520582", "body": "i believe this is coming from an un-checked `dev.Alias.GetName()` on an `api.HostDevice` type", "body_missing": false, "created_at": "2025-06-04T12:32:23Z", "id": 2939864975}, {"author": "person-43cbd26d", "body": "<!-- Answer -->
Your suspicion is correct: the KubeVirt codebase contains multiple places where dev.Alias.GetName() is called on api.HostDevice without checking if Alias is nil. The Alias field is a pointer, and the GetName() method does not perform a nil check, so if Alias is nil, this will cause a nil pointer dereference and a panic in virt-launcher. This risk is present in functions like detachHostDevices, deviceLookup, hostDevicesNames, and DifferenceHostDevicesByAlias, among others, where no guard is present before calling GetName() on the Alias pointer. If a host device is injected or constructed without an Alias (for example, via custom XML injection in a hook), this can easily trigger the crash you are seeing. 

In standard code paths, HostDevice objects are constructed with an Alias using api.NewUserDefinedAlias, so the dereference is safe. However, custom or external injection (such as your onDefineDomain hook) can bypass this, resulting in a nil Alias and a crash when GetName() is called later in the device handling logic. See the relevant code and usage patterns [here](https://github.com/kubevirt/kubevirt/blob/01584f5d4b140d3a528c0d4d2895a6099526c670/pkg/virt-launcher/virtwrap/device/hostdevice/hotplug.go).

To avoid this, ensure that any hostdev elements injected into the domain XML include an <alias> element, or that the corresponding api.HostDevice struct is initialized with a non-nil Alias. Alternatively, the codebase should be hardened with nil checks before calling GetName() on Alias pointers to prevent similar panics in the future.


